This setup is used to deploy VPC infrastructure on AWS using Terraform. This is the very first step to do before deploying AWS Services.
The infrastructure will prepare two public subnets for Application load balancer controller to function with internet facing, and NAT gateway to be used by the private subnets.
the private subnet will run the eks cluster including the worker nodes and control plane, which will be exposed using the private api endpoint - to communicate with each other - and public endpoint - for external connection with the cluster.

| Term                                | Meaning                                                                                      |
| ----------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **ALB (Application Load Balancer)** | AWS's load balancer that accepts traffic from the internet.                                  |
| **ALB Controller**                  | A tool in EKS that watches `Ingress` resources and automatically creates an ALB in AWS.      |
| **NGINX Ingress Controller**        | A pod inside your cluster that handles traffic **after** it enters Kubernetes.               |
| **Ingress**                         | A Kubernetes resource that defines rules like: "`/app → service A`", "`/admin → service B`". |
| **Owned ALB**                       | A **dedicated** ALB for a single Ingress (like one app or team).                             |
| **Shared ALB**                      | One ALB **shared** by multiple apps or Ingresses. Saves cost.                                |



| Case # | Setup Description                                        | ALB Ownership                                     | Number of ALBs           | How Apps Are Routed                                                   | DNS Endpoints                     | Use Case / Pros & Cons                                                                 |
| ------ | -------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **1**  | **Shared ALB directly routing to apps**                  | Shared (one ALB shared)                           | 1 ALB shared across apps | ALB routes directly to different app services based on paths/hosts    | Single ALB DNS shared by all apps | Cheaper (fewer ALBs), simpler external DNS; less isolation                             |
| **2**  | **Owned ALB per app, ALB routes directly to app**        | Owned (one ALB per app)                           | Multiple ALBs, 1 per app | Each ALB routes directly to one app                                   | Multiple ALB DNS, one per ALB     | Better isolation/security, more ALB cost and management overhead                       |
| **3**  | **Shared ALB routing to NGINX, NGINX routes internally** | Shared ALB (multiple ALB Ingresses share one ALB) | 1 ALB shared across apps | ALB routes all traffic to one NGINX; NGINX routes apps                | Single ALB DNS shared by all apps | Cost-efficient, central routing, flexible internal routing                             |
| **4**  | **Owned ALB routing to NGINX, NGINX routes internally**  | Owned (single ALB created by one ALB Ingress)     | One ALB per ALB Ingress  | One ALB routes all traffic to NGINX; NGINX routes all apps internally | One ALB DNS per ALB Ingress       | ALB ownership isolated, but traffic shared internally; hybrid of owned/shared concepts |



This infrastrucuture is dual stack, means it uses both ipv6 and ipv4. The IPv6 is used for Kubernetes stack while IPv4 is used to connect k8s to AWS ipv4 based services, as well to allow dual stack load balancer that recives traffic from both IP versions.